Chapter: RsaPssTest
  cites Foreword chapter Rsa
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 RSASSA-PSS signatures nobody here produced, checked here.

 TLS 1.3 requires this scheme and permits no substitute: RFC 8446 section
 4.4.3 says a CertificateVerify from an RSA certificate MUST be RSASSA-PSS.
 So this is not an alternative to the PKCS#1 v1.5 section next door, it is
 the only way an RSA server can ever be authenticated.

 Every signature below was made by OpenSSL 3.2.4 and every one was checked
 against .NET's RSA on Windows CNG before any of this chapter was written.
 Codex cannot produce a PSS signature and does not want to -- there is no
 signer here and no random number generator -- so there is no paired
 producer to agree with, and no way for this test to pass by two halves
 sharing a mistake.

 THE SALT IS RANDOM, which is what makes this scheme different to check
 and different to test. p-sig and p-sig2 are the same key over the same
 message and they are DIFFERENT BYTE STRINGS. Both must verify. A verifier
 that somehow depended on a particular salt would pass with one of them and
 is caught by having two.

 We say:

