 THE FORGERIES. These three are made with the private key directly, not
 by a signing library, because the two checks they isolate operate on the
 RECOVERED block -- which nobody who does not hold the key can steer. A
 signature is the one input an attacker chooses freely, so "an attacker
 cannot reach this" is not an argument; holding the key is exactly what
 the forger in the threat model does.

 All three encode the same message with the same fixed salt. p-sig-ctl is
 correct and MUST verify, which is what makes the other two evidence: they
 differ from it in one bit and one byte respectively, so a rejection can
 only be the check being aimed at. .NET rejects both forgeries and accepts
 the control.

