Section: Cases

  ok-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig
  ok-case2 : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig2

 The genuine signature against a different message.

  other-msg : List Integer = pss-flip p-msg 0
  wrong-msg-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e other-msg p-sig

 One bit of the signature. The first byte and the last are flipped
 separately: they fail for different reasons and through different code.
 A flip anywhere makes the recovered block effectively random, so what is
 actually being tested is that SOME check fires rather than which one.

  bad-first : List Integer = pss-flip p-sig 0
  bad-last : List Integer = pss-flip p-sig 255
  bad-first-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg bad-first
  bad-last-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg bad-last

 A signature of the wrong width, refused before any arithmetic.

  short-sig : List Integer = pss-take p-sig 255 0 []
  short-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg short-sig

Section: The Guards, Asserted Where They Can Fail

 THE SALT LENGTH. These two are the reason this section exists, and they
 are not synthetic: both are GENUINE PSS signatures over this exact
 message under this exact key, and OpenSSL verifies both at their own salt
 length. They differ from p-sig only in that the salt is zero bytes and
 forty-eight bytes rather than thirty-two.

 rsa_pss_rsae_sha256 fixes the salt at the digest length, so both must be
 refused. A verifier that recovers the salt length from the encoding
 instead of insisting on it -- which RFC 8017 permits and which is the
 easier thing to write -- accepts both, and accepts a zero-length salt in
 particular, which is a different scheme with a different security
 argument. No other line in this test would move.

  salt0-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-salt0
  salt48-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-salt48

 THE TWO SCHEMES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE, in both directions. p-sig-v15
 is a genuine PKCS#1 v1.5 signature over the same message under the same
 key. It must verify under v1.5 and must NOT verify under PSS; p-sig must
 do the opposite. Checking only one direction leaves a verifier that
 accepts everything from the right key regardless of scheme, which is what
 TLS 1.3 forbidding v1.5 in the handshake exists to prevent.

  v15-under-v15 : Boolean = rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-v15
  v15-under-pss : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-v15
  pss-under-v15 : Boolean = rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig
  pss-under-pss : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig

 THE TWO CHECKS ON THE RECOVERED BLOCK. Neither is reachable by altering
 a signature from outside: any change to the bytes makes the recovered
 block effectively random and it fails at the digest comparison instead,
 so with both checks deleted every other line in this test is unchanged.
 That was measured, not assumed.

 The control is the load-bearing line. Without it, two rejections prove
 only that hand-built signatures do not verify, which would be true of
 anything. With it, the construction is known good and each forgery
 differs from it in exactly one place.

 p-sig-topbit sets the top bit of the encoded message, which is not part
 of it: emBits is 2047 for this key and that bit lies outside, so RFC 8017
 step 6 requires it to be zero. Skipping the check makes a whole family of
 distinct encodings of one signature all verify.

 p-sig-sep changes the 0x01 that separates the padding from the salt to
 0x02, which step 10 requires.

  ctl-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-ctl
  topbit-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-topbit
  sep-case : Boolean = rsa-verify-pss-sha256 p-n p-e p-msg p-sig-sep

 MGF1 on its own, because every use of it above is buried under a
 signature check that fails for any reason at all. RFC 8017 appendix
 B.2.1 with a SHA-256 hash: the mask for an empty seed is the start of
 SHA-256 of the four counter bytes 00 00 00 00, so the first byte is a
 value that can be looked up rather than one this tree computed.

  mgf-empty : List Integer = rsa-mgf1 [] 4
  mgf-long : List Integer = rsa-mgf1 [1, 2, 3] 100

 The modulus bit length, which decides emLen and the free-bit mask. This
 modulus is 2048 bits and its top byte is 169, so the answer is 2048 and
 not, say, 2041.

  mod-bits : Integer = rsa-mod-bits p-n

Section: Helpers

  pss-take : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  pss-take (bs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else pss-take bs n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

  pss-copy : List Integer -> List Integer
  pss-copy (bs) = pss-take bs (list-length bs) 0 []

  pss-flip : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  pss-flip (bs) (i) = list-set-at (pss-copy bs) i (bit-xor (list-at bs i) 1)

  show-bytes : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  show-bytes (bs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length bs then acc
    else show-bytes bs (i + 1) (acc & " " & show (list-at bs i))

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("openssl pss signature verifies = " & show ok-case)
    print-line-uni ("a second signature, different salt = " & show ok-case2)
    print-line-uni ("signature against another message = " & show wrong-msg-case)
    print-line-uni ("first signature byte flipped = " & show bad-first-case)
    print-line-uni ("last signature byte flipped = " & show bad-last-case)
    print-line-uni ("signature of the wrong width = " & show short-case)
    print-line-uni ("genuine pss with a zero-length salt = " & show salt0-case)
    print-line-uni ("genuine pss with a 48-byte salt = " & show salt48-case)
    print-line-uni ("pkcs1 signature under pkcs1 = " & show v15-under-v15)
    print-line-uni ("pkcs1 signature under pss = " & show v15-under-pss)
    print-line-uni ("pss signature under pkcs1 = " & show pss-under-v15)
    print-line-uni ("pss signature under pss = " & show pss-under-pss)
    print-line-uni ("hand-built control signature = " & show ctl-case)
    print-line-uni ("control with the top bit set = " & show topbit-case)
    print-line-uni ("control with the separator 0x02 = " & show sep-case)
    print-line-uni ("mgf1 of the empty seed, 4 bytes =" & show-bytes mgf-empty 0 "")
    print-line-uni ("mgf1 length for 100 requested = " & show (list-length mgf-long))
    print-line-uni ("modulus bit length = " & show mod-bits)
  end

